        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The defect\n\n`parser.py` has only this:\n\n```python\ndef parse_enabled(value):\n    return bool(value)\n```\n\n`bool()` checks whether a value is empty, not what it says. In Python, any non-empty string counts as true, so this function gets the main case wrong and never raises an error. I ran it to confirm:\n\n| Input | Returns | Should |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"true\"` | `True` | `True` ✅ |\n| `\"false\"` | **`True`** | `False` ❌ |\n| `\"no\"`, `\"0\"`, `\"False\"` | **`True`** | raise `ValueError` ❌ |\n| `\"\"`, `None` | `False` | raise `ValueError` ❌ |\n\nThe worst case is `\"false\"`, which turns the setting **on**. Anyone who tries to disable it gets the opposite result. Other bad input is accepted without any error.\n\n## Recommended fix\n\nI didn't change any files. A fix would look like this:\n\n```python\ndef parse_enabled(value):\n    if value == \"true\":\n        return True\n    if value == \"false\":\n        return False\n    raise ValueError(f\"expected 'true' or 'false', got {value!r}\")\n```\n\n## Preventing a repeat\n\nThe best protection is an automated check rather than a written rule. The repo currently has only `parser.py`, with no tests and no CI (automated checks that run on each change). I'm suggesting these rather than adding them, since you asked for no changes:\n\n1. **A test file, `test_parser.py`, run with pytest.** It should check that `\"true\"` returns `True`, that `\"false\"` returns `False`, and that inputs like `\"False\"`, `\"TRUE\"`, `\" true\"`, `\"0\"`, `\"no\"`, `\"\"`, `None`, `1` and `True` all raise `ValueError`. The current code would fail this test on `\"false\"` straight away.\n2. **Run that test automatically**, either in a pre-commit hook or in CI, so a change like this can't be merged without someone noticing.\n3. **Optionally, add type hints and a type checker.** Declaring `value: str -> bool` and running mypy or pyright helps document the expected input. It wouldn't have caught this bug on its own, though, so the test is what matters.\n\nWant me to add the fix and the test?"
        }
